            <ArticleTitle>Exercise treadmill test may predict clinical outcome among working-aged patients suspected of coronary heart disease in general practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>47-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To specify the diagnostic value of treadmill tests conducted in primary health care by trained GPs for working-aged patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A two-year follow-up study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Kangasala Health Centre, Finland.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">All patients under the age of 60 (n = 348) examined by exercise treadmill test carried out by trained GPs during a period of three years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">The number of patients having a diagnosis of coronary heart disease at the end of two years' follow-up. Predictive values of the exercise test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">During the two years' follow-up period CHD was diagnosed in six of the patients who did not yield a positive finding in the test. A negative finding in the test was very reliable; coronary heart disease manifested in only 2% of these cases. Almost half of the patients yielding a positive finding ended up having coronary angiography and 1 in 10 underwent coronary bypass surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Among working-aged patients suspected of coronary heart disease an exercise treadmill test may predict clinical outcome in general practice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
